Date: Sun, 10 Jan 93 1:28:52 CST
From: Michael R Linksvayer <mrl28033@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu>
Subject: We aren't doomed to argue in circles

Tim May wondered in a recent post if we have discussed
everything there is to discuss and are doomed to repeat the
same arguments over the same subjects ad nauseum.

For some topics, which I will call "philosophic" (describing
the universe as it exists) I think that exhaustive repetition
is inevitable. We are not going to discover anything new about
the Fermi Paradox or ethics in this forum.

Other topics, which I will call "technological" (utilizing the
universe), offer an endless supply of fodder for new
discussion.  There are many new ideas yet to be created in
fields such as artificial life, cryptography, idea futures,
intelligence increase, life extension, PPL, spontaneous orders,
etc. (Note that I am using the word technology in a very broad
sense. I think social technologies are at least as important as
new gadgets.)

I find the philosophy discussions interesting. Occasionally
someone introduces an idea that I was ignorant of before, or
more often presents a new (to me) way of looking at an old
idea.

The subjects of technology discussions are interesting,
exciting, and important.  Exciting because we can do something
about them, wheras we cannot alter the nature of the universe.
Important because they have an impact on my survival. If I do
not survive then whether we are the only intelligent life in
the universe or we are being simulated in someone's VR or
anything inbetween does not mean squat to me.

