Date: Sun, 10 Jan 93 10:23:17 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re:  Gray Goo explained

Mark Pickens suggests that a spaceship with most of its matter in the
form of computers running a VR could house many more people than if
they were physically present in the ship.

This is possible, but not certain.  Mark points out that there is a
tradeoff between space and time in computers, so perhaps by running
the VR sufficiently slowly we could get arbitrarily large numbers of
people?  I'd make two points, though.  First, even the non-running
("swapped out") people need to be stored somewhere, so there would
still be some limits on the number of people the ship could hold.

Second, perhaps a better measure is person-seconds for a given voyage,
so if the VR actually had to run slower than real time it should get
penalized.  This would make sense in several contexts, such as a
competitive economy, or simply a world in which people wanted to get as
much living done as possible before the heat death of the universe.

The other argument I would offer in favor of real space is the
possibility of engineering people to make them smaller.  Robin Hanson
posted here several months ago his idea for shrinking people to about 1/3
of an inch in height, using nanotech computers for their brains.  This
would reduce the advantage VR would have over physical space.

Physical space can be thought of as a network of simple cellular-automata
type computers, with sub-nuclear size and time cycles.  I just have this
intuitive sense that it's going to be hard to beat this much computing
power with the cruder molecular computers we are talking about.

Hal

