From: zane@ddsw1.mcs.com (Sameer Parekh)
Subject: Re: Why is socialism attractive?

In message <9301100013.AA08121@albert.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, Nick Szabo writes:
> The global economy is a very different culture than the hunter-gatherer 
> tribe.  In the latter, people often have a lifelong, face-to-face 
> relationship.  They can perform altruistic acts with the (perhaps 
> unconscious) assurance that such altruism will, more often than not, 
> benefit kin or be reciprocated down the line.  
	I agree mostly with your post, but I think that this "tribal
relationship" is possible among certain groups of people, even without
ftf relations. One mailing list I am on is a form of a family, in which
I feel that I could perform semi-altruistic (in which the only thing I
want in return is the knowledge that I helped the person I'm helping and
he/she appreciates it.) acts for these people, even without ftf
connection. (Many of us have met ftf, but before we met ftf I still felt
this relation.)
	I think that through the technology, we can expand on the tribal
metaphor. It would be different than earlier, restrictive, h-g forms of
society, in that there is that element of personal choice. I'm speaking
on a small scale, very personal scale here, while your post *was* more
on the global scale.
	The "global village" idea relating to television doesn't work for
me because it isn't interactive like a village. The Internet, however,
can be that village because there *is* interaction among the
participants. Television is merely a means of providing a product (the
viewers) to the client (the advertisers) while the Internet is a true
"global community" in which strong bonds can be formed.
	Extending the metaphor of the Internet->village, one might say that
the usenet is like the public square, with people shouting and hardly
anyone listening, and mailing lists are like cafes, in which small
groups of people get together and converse. (The list I mentioned above
is more like a "home.")

