I think there are three reasons socialism has such an appeal:

1.  It appeals to the universal human desire to get
    something for nothing.
2.  Wishful thinking.  (NOT the same as 1.  See below.)
3.  It works beautifully--in the short-term.

ONE--All of us, including Extropians, want to get something for
nothing, or at least for as little effort as possible.  That's
why we negotiate, that's why we replaced human toil with that of
animals; and then replaced that with mechanical power.  Socialism
promises that at least some folks will be taken care of, whether
they contribute or not.  That's mighty appealing to most people.

TWO--The belief that you will be taken care of IF you ever need
it isn't enough to make socialism really appealing.  In addition,
you need to believe you WILL win the socialism lottery.  Wishful
thinking allows every socialist to rationalize that he or she is
a"special case" and, therefore, DESERVES to be carried on
everyone else's back.

THREE--Socialism works in the short-term for two reasons:  The
"honeymoon" syndrome and the "Empire State Building" syndrome.

"Honeymoon."  Whenever new enterprises start--countries,
businesses, communes or marriages--everyone is optimistic and
full of enthusiasm for the project.  Under those conditions,
people will often put out extraordinary effort to achieve the
goals of the enterprise.  After the initial "glow" has worn off,
however, they begin to slow down.  They might also notice that
others have slowed down, as well.  Eventually, they will begin to
resent the "slackers" and then slack off even further themselves,
"just to make things fair, you understand."  Look how long it
took Sweden and New Zealand to start re-thinking their love
affairs with socialism.

"Empire State Building."  You know the joke about the man who
falls off the building, and is heard to say as he passes the 57th
floor, "So far, so good!"  When countries start to introduce
socialist policies, they usually do so in the context of a
pre-existing free market sector of the economy.  It takes time to
"eat the seed corn" that more-or-less free markets have created.
Socialism looks very much like a free lunch during the relatively
long period it can take to consume a nations accumulated capital.
The Soviet Union took three-quarters of a century to squander the
industrialization of the Czars, the factories of the Germans, the
resources of the captive nations and the largesse of the West.
But eventually the party had to come to an end.  How did the Evil
Empire die?  Not with a bang, but a whimper.

In the meantime, don't think the socialists have gone away.  I
have spoken with a few, and their denial mechanisms are working
overtime.  For a good laugh, find a socialist.  He will have no
problem telling you why he's GLAD the USSR fell, and how its
demise VINDICATES "true" socialism, the wave of the future.

