From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@ra.cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Re:  SELF/LING:  Speed reading

   I took a speed-reading class a while ago.
The techniques I was taught were of two kinds:
 
- focusing you attention:
  by the end of the class, you would almost automatically remember
  the author and the title of the thing you read, and a few main ideas
  and facts. Also, little details of thoughts, etc.
  My class was mostly focusing on technical details, but I believe
  similar questions could help you better concentrate on images and
  feelings of non-technical piece.
 
- physical aspects of reading:
  in many cases, you could absorb the text faster, but you visual system
  is a bottleneck.
  the exercises would help you improve peripheral vision (allows to read
  more text in without moving the eyes), avoid 'regression' - involuntary
  returning to previously read words/lines, help stop whispering the words
  you read, etc.
 
  - This is just what I remember.
  I considered the course very useful, though not miraculous.
  As I remember, my 'effective reading speed' increased by about 200+%
  (they measured it as a raw speed * percentage of questions about the
  text you could answer after reading it).
 
  I don't think *any* course can teach you *read* NY Times in 3-4 minutes.
Scan, maybe.  You just cannot process information that fast.
(At least, I can't :(  )
  With fiction, I think it's still more difficult.  You (I) can't feel
faster, no matter how fast you read or even think.
Just like in real life. Maybe, I could smell flowers in 0.5 seconds,
kiss in 0.2 seconds, etc. - but would I?

